% === provenance: LoiblRummel2013 ===
% CLAIM: Delaying instruction is not by itself what makes problem solving
%   before instruction work; the instruction phase must compare and contrast
%   the students' own solutions with the canonical one.  Without that,
%   problem solving first is no better than instruction first.
% FOUND-VIA: scholar search '"problem solving prior to instruction" learning'
%   -n iterations-productive-failure -p openalex -p dblp -p wos -p scopus
%   --limit 40 (query S2 of bilaga C).
% PICKED: the experiment that isolates the mechanism, and therefore the one
%   that decides how this material must be written; chosen over the authors'
%   2014 journal paper because that one is closed access and this one is the
%   study that varies the consolidation phase directly.
% QUOTE (abstract, p. 296): "Our results indicate that comparing and
%   contrasting typical student solutions is a prerequisite for the
%   effectiveness of problem-solving prior to instruction. Problem-solving
%   prior to instruction combined with instruction where student solutions
%   were compared and contrasted to the canonical solution outperformed all
%   other conditions. Problem-solving prior to instruction was no more
%   effective than standard instruction prior to problem-solving."
% QUOTE (results, pp. 299--300): "for conceptual knowledge, problem-solving
%   prior to instruction was only more effective than instruction prior to
%   problem solving if student solutions were compared and contrasted during
%   the instruction."
% VERIFIED: full text read (ISLS repository copy, retrieved through a
%   Wayback Machine snapshot after the live repository returned errors).
%   Applies because it separates "delay the telling" from "contrast the
%   attempts", which is precisely the design choice this material makes.
% COUNTER: this entry IS the counter-evidence to the unqualified form of the
%   claim; the corresponding support is Kapur2014 and SinhaKapur2021.
% DATE: 2026-09-03
% FOUND-VIA (here, tutorial): copied verbatim from
%   modules/iterations/slides/ltnotes.bib; the claim is backed in
%   föreläsningen Upprepningar, bilaga C (produktivt misslyckande).
@inproceedings{LoiblRummel2013,
  author    = {Loibl, Katharina and Rummel, Nikol},
  title     = {Delaying Instruction Alone Doesn't Work: Comparing and
               Contrasting Student Solutions is Necessary for Learning from
               Problem-Solving Prior to Instruction},
  booktitle = {Proceedings of the 10th International Conference on Computer
               Supported Collaborative Learning (CSCL 2013)},
  pages     = {296--303},
  year      = {2013},
  publisher = {International Society of the Learning Sciences},
}

